# 5. Comparative Evaluation Matrix

Once the individual pillar assessments are complete, each major policy option should be evaluated using a common framework.

| Criterion                 | Core Question                                                               |
| ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Ecological effectiveness  | Is there evidence the intervention will improve ecological outcomes?        |
| Institutional feasibility | Can responsible institutions actually implement it?                         |
| Legal feasibility         | Does existing authority support it, or is new legislation required?         |
| Fiscal implications       | What resources are required?                                                |
| Administrative burden     | How difficult is implementation?                                            |
| Distributional effects    | Who bears costs and who receives benefits?                                  |
| Durability                | Is the intervention likely to survive political and institutional turnover? |
| Adaptability              | Can it respond to new information and changing conditions?                  |
| Measurability             | Can meaningful outcomes be tracked?                                         |
| Uncertainty               | How strong is the evidence base?                                            |
| Scalability               | Can successful approaches be expanded?                                      |
| Reversibility             | Can the intervention be modified or discontinued if it fails?               |

This matrix should inform later recommendations without turning the project into a simplistic numerical scoring exercise.

